---
references:
  book:
    id: book
    type: book
    title: A Book
    authors:
      - firstName: John
        lastName: Smith
    language: en
---

The citation head is `[^reference_id]`: the caret is the slot tag and the bare
content is the reference (§7.3). Every other position is an ordinary field of the
author's — the bracket's quote included — so a head never loses content for want
of a preset row, and an adjacent extras head decorates the node without ever
writing the reference (§6.2):

Minim [^book "hlm. 45"]@@citeX{.paper, #smith, note: "short"} esse do ut anim proident.

`locator` is not a cite field any more, so an extras `locator:` prop is an ordinary
prop like any other — the head's quote stays `bracket.quote`:

[^book "hlm. 45"]@@citeX{locator: "kept", .thin}

`citation_number` is stable by the reference alone (`stable_fields` defaults to
`["reference_id"]`), so a second mention keeps the number whatever else differs:

[^book]@@citeX{.repeat}

A repeated citation keeps its number; only its anchor is new (`cite-<n>`):

[^book]@@citeX{.again}

Every position the preset does not name stays a bare field, so a paren's contents
are ordinary fields of the node (§6.1, D-7):

[^book "hlm. 7"](paperback "Reprint")@@citeX

A head without a reference is an error, not a citation (§7.3): `[^]` and `[^"book"]`
stay text, and so does a citation of a reference the document does not know. The
retired `[^^](book "hlm. 45")` spelling is not a citation either — its second caret
is now the reference:

[^] and [^"book"] and [^nosuch]
